      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "In C++17, a function signature is declared as `void updateRecord(Student s)` where `Student` is a `struct` containing a 1024-byte array. What happens when a caller invokes `updateRecord(myStudent)`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "A copy of `myStudent` is constructed for `s`, so changes to `s` inside `updateRecord` do not modify `myStudent` in the caller."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "The memory address of `myStudent` is passed implicitly because `struct` instances decay to pointers just like raw C++ arrays."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "The compiler rejects the function call because `struct` types containing fixed-size arrays cannot be passed by value in C++17."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Changes to `s` inside `updateRecord` are copied back over `myStudent` in the caller upon function return via copy-restore."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Passing this simple Student struct by value creates a separate parameter object, including copies of its fixed-size array members. Mutating those members does not change the caller object. C++17 does not mandate storage in a particular stack frame.",
