      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Consider this mathematical procedure on an arbitrary positive integer n, using exact unbounded integer arithmetic: stop if n = 1; otherwise replace n by n/2 when n is even, or by 3n+1 when n is odd, and repeat. Assume that universal termination has not been proved. Which algorithm property would a proof of the Collatz conjecture establish for this procedure? This is mathematical pseudocode, not a bounded C++17 implementation.",
