      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "In dynamic array resizing, if an array doubles at `n = cap` (becoming 1/2 full) and immediately halves when `n = cap - 1`, an alternating sequence of insertions and deletions at that boundary incurs $\\Theta(n)$ work on every operation, thrashing worst-case performance. By waiting until `n <= cap / 4` before halving to `cap / 2`, the array remains at least 1/4 full and amortized cost per operation stays $O(1)$.",
