      "stem": "Compare a Binary Max-Heap with a **balanced** Binary Search Tree (such as an AVL tree, which maintains `left < parent < right` and height $O(\\log n)$). Why does searching for an arbitrary key `x` take $\\Theta(n)$ worst-case time in a Max-Heap instead of $O(\\log n)$?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Both a Max-Heap and a balanced BST maintain `left < parent < right` and support $O(\\log n)$ arbitrary-key search."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "A Max-Heap enforces `left < parent < right` only when the number of nodes is odd."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "A Max-Heap only keeps each parent `>=` its children, with no left-versus-right order; when both children are larger than `x`, either side may hold `x`, so both must be searched."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Finding even the maximum element in a Max-Heap takes $O(n)$ time because the maximum resides at a leaf."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "In a balanced BST (`left < parent < right`, height $O(\\log n)$), one comparison with `x` tells which single subtree can contain `x`, so search takes $O(\\log n)$. A Max-Heap only orders parents above children. A heap search can still skip a subtree whose root is smaller than `x` (every key below it is smaller too), but when both children are larger than `x`, nothing says which side holds `x`, so both must be searched. Searching for a key smaller than every key in the heap (and not present) visits all `n` nodes: $\\Theta(n)$ worst case.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "A Max-Heap satisfies `parent >= child` (vertical heap property), not the horizontal BST property `left < parent < right`.",
        "B": "Heap ordering never changes based on node-count parity; it never orders left vs right siblings globally.",
        "D": "The global maximum of a Max-Heap is always at the root (`peekMax()` takes $O(1)$ time)."
      },
