      "question_category": "tracing_application",
      "stem": "Why is `log_2(n)` in $\\Theta(\\log_{10}(n))$ (meaning the logarithm base can be omitted inside $O(\\log n)$ or $\\Theta(\\log n)$ when the base is a constant `b > 1`), whereas $2^n$ is NOT in $O(3^n)$'s inverse (i.e., $3^n$ is NOT in $O(2^n)$)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`log_2(n)` and `log_10(n)` evaluate to the exact same numerical value for all integers `n >= 2`, whereas $3^n$ and $2^n$ differ by `n`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "$3^n$ is actually in $\\Theta(2^n)$ because all exponential functions with constant bases belong to the exact same asymptotic growth class."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "By base change, `log_2(n) = (1 / log_10(2)) * log_10(n)` differs by a constant factor, whereas $3^n / 2^n = (1.5)^n$ grows to infinity."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Base changes are ignored in both logarithms and exponentials."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Changing the base of a logarithm multiplies the function by a constant factor `1 / log_b(a)`, which is absorbed by Big-O/Theta. In contrast, changing the base of an exponential from $2^n$ to $3^n$ multiplies by $(3/2)^n \\to \\infty$, so $3^n$ is strictly larger than $O(2^n)$ (and $2^{2n} = 4^n$ is strictly larger than $O(2^n)$).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`log_2(n) approx 3.322 * log_10(n)`; they differ by a constant multiplier.",
        "B": "Different exponential bases ($2^n$ vs $3^n$) belong to completely different asymptotic complexity classes.",
        "D": "Never ignore the base of an exponential function."
      },
