      "stem": "An inversion in an array `A` is an ordered pair of indices `(p, q)` such that `p < q` and `A[p] > A[q]`. In the array `A = [5, 2, 4, 1, 3]`, how many inversions exist, and how many element right-shifts (`A[j+1] = A[j]`) does Insertion Sort perform to sort `A`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "There are `7` inversions, but Insertion Sort performs only `4` element right-shifts (`n - 1`)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "There are `10` inversions, and Insertion Sort performs `10` element right-shifts (`5 * 4 / 2`)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "There are `7` inversions, and Insertion Sort performs `7` element right-shifts."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "There are `5` inversions and `5` shifts."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "In Insertion Sort (`while (j >= 0 && A[j] > key) A[j+1] = A[j];`), `A[j]` is shifted right past `key = A[i]` if and only if `(j, i)` was an inversion (`j < i` and `A[j] > A[i]`). Therefore, the total number of shifts performed by Insertion Sort is identically equal to the number of inversions `I` in the input array (`7` here: `i=1(2): 1`, `i=2(4): 1`, `i=3(1): 3`, `i=4(3): 2` -> `1+1+3+2 = 7`), and total running time is $\\Theta(n + I)$.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Every single inversion requires one shift of `A[j]` past `key`.",
        "B": "`10 = 5*4/2` is the maximum possible inversions for a strictly reverse-sorted array `[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]`.",
        "D": "Miscounts the inversions involving `1` and `3`."
      },
