      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "On a random permutation, Lomuto swaps half of the elements (`n / 2` swaps), whereas Hoare only swaps when an element in the left part is `>= pivot` and an element in the right part is `<= pivot` (`n / 6` swaps on average—3x fewer swaps.). Furthermore, because Hoare stops both pointers on elements equal to `pivot` (`A[i] >= pivot, A[j] <= pivot`), an array of all-equal elements splits cleanly in half ($\\Theta(n \\log n)$) instead of Lomuto's `0 : n-1` split ($\\Theta(n^2)$).",
