      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Classic B-trees store bulky data records inside both internal nodes and leaves, which (1) reduces how many separator keys fit in an internal disk page (lowering branching factor `t`), and (2) forces a range scan `[L, R]` to alternate between leaves and internal nodes. B+ trees move all records to the leaves (maximizing internal fanout `t`) and link the leaves into a sequential chain for fast range scans.",
