      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "Which of the following sets of conditions is necessary and sufficient for an undirected graph `G = (V, E)` with `|V| = n >= 1` vertices to be a tree (a connected acyclic undirected graph)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`G` is connected and every vertex has degree `<= 2`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`G` has `|E| = n - 1` edges (without requiring `G` to be connected or acyclic)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`G` is connected and has `|E| = n - 1` edges."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`G` is bipartite and connected."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "By the fundamental characterization of trees, any two of the three properties (1) connected, (2) acyclic, and (3) `|E| = |V| - 1` on an undirected graph `G = (V, E)` mathematically imply the third, uniquely defining a tree.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "A star graph `K_{1,5}` is a tree where the center vertex has degree 5 (`> 2`); conversely, a simple cycle `C_n` has all degrees equal to 2 (`<= 2`) and is connected, yet is not a tree.",
        "B": "Having `n - 1` edges alone is NOT sufficient. For `n = 4`, a triangle `(1,2,3)` plus an isolated vertex `4` has `3 = n - 1` edges but is disconnected and cyclic.",
        "D": "A 4-cycle `C_4` is connected and bipartite (even cycle) but is not a tree."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "MC_TREE_N_MINUS_ONE_EDGES_ALONE_INSUFFICIENT"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
