      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "In a directed graph `G = (V, E)`, what is the difference between `G` being strongly connected and `G` being weakly connected?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`G` is strongly connected only if it is a complete directed graph with `n(n-1)` edges."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`G` is strongly connected if every vertex satisfies `deg^-(v) == deg^+(v)`, and weakly connected if every vertex has $\\deg^-(v) \\ge 1$."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`G` is strongly connected if it contains no directed cycles (a DAG)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Strongly connected: every pair `(u, v)` has paths both ways; weakly connected: connected when edge directions are ignored."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Strong connectivity requires mutual directed reachability (`u ~> v` and `v ~> u`) for all pairs `u, v in V`. Weak connectivity only requires connectivity in the underlying undirected graph (ignoring edge orientations).",
