      "stem": "Given a directed graph `G = (V, E)` in outgoing Adjacency List form `adj[0..V-1]`, we want to construct its transpose (reverse) graph $G^T = (V, E^T)$ where `E^T = {(v, u) : (u, v) in E}` using:\n```cpp\nstd::vector<std::vector<int>> adjT(V);\nfor (int u = 0; u < V; ++u) {\n    for (int v : adj[u]) {\n        adjT[v].push_back(u);\n    }\n}\n```\nWhat is the exact time and space complexity of constructing `adjT`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "$\\Theta(V + E)$ time and $\\Theta(V + E)$ auxiliary space."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "$\\Theta(V^2)$ worst-case time and $\\Theta(V^2)$ auxiliary space."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "$\\Theta(V \\cdot E)$ time and $\\Theta(V^2)$ auxiliary space."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "$\\Theta(E \\log V)$ time."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Allocating `adjT(V)` takes $\\Theta(V)$ time and space. The outer loop runs `V` times, and the inner loop executes $\\deg^+(u)$ times for each `u`, performing an amortized $O(1)$ `push_back`. Since $\\sum_{u \\in V} \\deg^+(u) = E$, the total work is $\\Theta(V + E)$—a key building block of Kosaraju's SCC algorithm.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "$\\Theta(V^2)$ applies to transposing an Adjacency Matrix, not an Adjacency List.",
        "C": "A classic nested-loop analysis trap: the inner loop runs $\\deg^+(u)$ times, not `E` times per vertex; the sum over all `u` is `E`, not `V * E`.",
        "D": "`push_back` on `std::vector` does not perform sorting or binary search ($\\log V$)."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "hard",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "MC_GRAPH_TRANSPOSE_NESTED_LOOP_SUM"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
