      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "In a DFS forest of a directed graph `G = (V, E)` with timestamps `(d[u], f[u])` for every vertex, suppose we know that `(u, v) in E` is a directed edge and we observe that `f[u] < f[v]` (`u` finished *before* `v` finished). Which type(s) of edge can `(u, v)` possibly be?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`(u, v)` can be any of the four edge types."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`(u, v)` must be a Tree edge or a Forward edge (since descendants always finish after their ancestors: `f[v] >= f[u]`)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`(u, v)` must be a Cross edge."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`(u, v)` must be a Back edge (since for all Tree, Forward, and Cross edges in a DFS forest, `f[u] > f[v]` holds)."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Check all 4 edge types for `(u, v)`:\n- Tree or Forward edge: `v` is a proper descendant of `u`, so `[d[v], f[v]] subset [d[u], f[u]]`, meaning `d[u] < d[v] < f[v] < f[u]` (`f[u] > f[v]`).\n- Cross edge: when `u` explores `(u, v)`, `v` is already `BLACK` (finished), so `f[v] < d[u] < f[u]` (`f[u] > f[v]`).\n- Back edge: `u` is a descendant of `v`, so `d[v] <= d[u] < f[u] <= f[v]` (`f[u] <= f[v]`).\nThus `f[u] < f[v]` holds if and only if `(u, v)` is a Back edge. (And consequently, in any DAG, *every* edge `(u, v)` satisfies `f[u] > f[v]`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Only Back edges have `f[u] <= f[v]`.",
        "B": "For Tree and Forward edges, `v` is a descendant of `u`, so `v` finishes *before* `u` (`f[v] < f[u]`).",
        "C": "For a Cross edge `(u, v)`, `v` is already `BLACK` when `u` inspects `(u, v)`, so `f[v] < d[u] < f[u]`."
      },
