      "learning_objective_id": "T13_1_LO2",
      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "In Interval Dynamic Programming (such as Matrix Chain Multiplication or Optimal Binary Search Tree, where state `dp[i][j]` represents the optimal cost for subsegment `i..j` with `i <= j` and transition `dp[i][j] = min_{i <= k < j} (dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + cost(i, k, j))`), why does the naive loop order `for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j)` FAIL?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Iterating `i` from `1` to `n` accesses `dp[i][k]` with `k > j` out of bounds; instead, iterate `i` from `1` to `n` and `j` from `n` down to `i`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Computing `dp[i][j]` needs `dp[k + 1][j]` where `k + 1 > i` (a future row not yet computed); instead, iterate by interval length `len = j - i + 1` from `2` to `n`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Interval Dynamic Programming recurrences can only be evaluated via top-down recursion and cannot be written with bottom-up `for` loops."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "The naive loop `i = 0..n-1, j = i+1..n-1` actually works because `k + 1 < i`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
