      "question_category": "code_trace",
      "stem": "In the classic Activity Selection (Interval Scheduling) problem, you are given `n` intervals `[s_i, f_i)` (`s_i < f_i`) and want to select a maximum-size subset of mutually non-overlapping intervals (where `[s_i, f_i)` and `[s_j, f_j)` are compatible if `f_i <= s_j` or `f_j <= s_i`). Which greedy sorting criterion is provably globally optimal?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Sort intervals in ascending order of earliest start time (`s_i`), and repeatedly select the next non-overlapping interval."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Sort by earliest finish time `f_i` ascending, and repeatedly pick the next interval with start `s_i >=` last selected finish time."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Sort intervals in ascending order of shortest duration (`f_i - s_i`), and repeatedly select the next non-overlapping interval."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Sort by fewest overlaps with other intervals ascending."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Picking the compatible activity with the earliest finish time `f_1 = min_i f_i` frees up the resource as early as possible, leaving the maximum possible remaining time window `[f_1, +infty)` for subsequent activities (verified by exchange argument: replacing the first activity `a_k` of any optimal schedule with `a_1` preserves compatibility since `f_1 <= f_k <= s_{next}`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Counterexample for earliest start time: `[0, 100)` starts earliest (`s = 0`) and blocks `[1, 2), [2, 3)` (`1` interval instead of `2`).",
        "C": "Counterexample for shortest duration: `[0, 5), [4, 7), [6, 11)`—the shortest interval `[4, 7)` (length `3`) overlaps both `[0, 5)` and `[6, 11)`, picking `1` interval instead of the optimal `2` (`{[0, 5), [6, 11)}`).",
        "D": "Fewest overlaps also fails on carefully constructed 11-interval counterexamples and takes $O(n^2)$ just to count overlaps."
      },
