      "question_category": "concept_invariant",
      "stem": "How can a Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) `T` of a complete weighted graph `G` (with non-negative edge weights) be used to provide a mathematically proven lower bound on the optimal TSP tour cost `OPT_TSP`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "The Minimum Spanning Tree weight `w(MST)` provides an upper bound on `OPT_TSP` (`OPT_TSP <= w(MST)`) rather than a lower bound."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "The Minimum Spanning Tree weight `w(MST)` is always strictly greater than `OPT_TSP` because a tree has more edges than a cycle."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`w(MST)` always equals `OPT_TSP / 2`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Deleting any edge `e` from an optimal TSP tour $C^*$ leaves a spanning tree, so $w(\\mathrm{MST}) \\le w(C^* - \\{e\\}) \\le \\mathrm{OPT}_{\\mathrm{TSP}}$."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Let $C^*$ be an optimal Hamiltonian cycle of cost `OPT_TSP`. Removing any one edge $e \\in C^*$ yields a Hamiltonian path `P`, which connects all `n` vertices with `n - 1` edges and no cycles—hence `P` is a spanning tree of `G`. By definition of MST, `w(MST) <= w(P) = OPT_TSP - w(e) <= OPT_TSP`. This makes `w(MST)` (and the sharper 1-tree bound) a fast $O(E \\log V)$ lower bound for evaluating how close a heuristic TSP tour is to optimal.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`w(MST)` is a lower bound (`w(MST) <= OPT_TSP`); doubling the MST (`2 * w(MST)`) gives an upper bound in Metric TSP.",
        "B": "Since $C^* - \\{e\\}$ is a spanning tree, the *minimum* spanning tree cannot weigh more than $C^* - \\{e\\}$.",
        "C": "`w(MST)` is at most `OPT_TSP` (and in Metric TSP, `OPT_TSP <= 2 * w(MST)`), not an exact equality."
      },
